+/*  ChopGimp: rendering jobs (background rendering and drafts)
+ *
+ *  A job renders one area of the projection (a few short slices, each a
+ *  GEGL blit) into memory of its own, at full resolution or, for a draft,
+ *  at a mipmap level > 0.  The main thread then copies the result into
+ *  the projection buffer and emits the update.
+ *
+ *  - Background rendering: the job runs in the rendering thread
+ *    (gimp-gegl-graph-lock.c), taking the graph lock around each slice;
+ *    the main thread keeps the projection buffer and all of the
+ *    projection's state to itself.
+ *
+ *  - Drafts (fast preview): while the user acts on the image, the changed
+ *    areas are rendered at a reduced resolution and written at the level
+ *    the display reads (upscaled at 100 % zoom); they are re-rendered at
+ *    full resolution once the image stops changing.  When the display is
+ *    zoomed out, the visible area is first rendered at the displayed
+ *    level, then refined.  Only graphs whose operations scale correctly
+ *    with the level are drafted (gimp_gegl_graph_is_draftable()).  At
+ *    100 % zoom, graphs with layer effects or blurs are not drafted
+ *    (4.2.7, see gimp_projection_job_new()).
+ *
+ *  Without either, the projection is rendered by the original code path
+ *  (gimp_projection_chunk_render_step()).
